    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nWe can represent mixed numbers with number lines.\n\nContent:\nFor example, let's write the number shown on the number line below as a mixed number.\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/fb88c92d4c5779868600d0189699d514]\n\nContent:\nFirst, notice that the line segment from [MATH: 0] to [MATH: 1] is split into [MATH: \\color{blue}3] equal parts.\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/b5df9d555e3e5d2465966022cfc4426a]\n\nContent:\nWe can land on our point by making [MATH: \\color{red}1] whole step plus [MATH: \\color{blue}2] part steps (out of a possible [MATH: {\\color{blue}{3}}).]\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/8e350f825fa881f5181f90439808a8dd]\n\nContent:\nTherefore, the mixed number is [MATH: {\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{3}}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Identifying Mixed Numbers Using Number Lines\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat mixed number is shown on the number line?\n\n[IMG: /graphics/c5d5506c4844efccf3a73c2f6fa59722]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nThe line segment from [MATH: 0] to [MATH: 1] is split into [MATH: \\color{blue}6] equal parts. Each part represents [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}6}] of a whole.\n\n[IMG: /graphics/1cf32f123e3f1f58047b7226300ad5f1]\n\nStarting from zero, we land on our point by making [MATH: \\color{red}2] whole steps plus [MATH: \\color{blue}1] part step:\n\n[IMG: /graphics/94e3752ec43416082cc2e879baa027fc]\n\nTherefore, the mixed number is [MATH: {\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{6}}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nQuestion Graphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/q-56649]\n\nPrompt:\nWhat mixed number is shown on the number line?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2(1)/(3)]\nb. [MATH: 3(1)/(2)]\nc. [MATH: 7(1)/(2)]\nd. [MATH: 2(1)/(2)]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(2)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nQuestion Graphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/q-56972]\n\nPrompt:\nWhat mixed number is shown on the number line?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2(1)/(2)]\nb. [MATH: 4(1)/(4)]\nc. [MATH: 2(3)/(4)]\nd. [MATH: 2(1)/(4)]\ne. [MATH: 3(1)/(4)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Identifying Mixed Numbers With Non-Unit Fractional Parts\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat mixed number is shown on the number line?\n\n[IMG: /graphics/ad95ce2f27751c9f5670050fc0f37a9e]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nThe line segment from [MATH: 0] to [MATH: 1] is split into [MATH: \\color{blue}4] equal parts. Each part represents [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}4}] of a whole.\n\n[IMG: /graphics/4ae3565a5ac4a7f72efd178a58d7f08b]\n\nStarting from zero, we land on our point by making [MATH: \\color{red}2] whole steps plus [MATH: \\color{blue}3] part steps (out of a possible [MATH: {\\color{blue}{4}}]).\n\n[IMG: /graphics/0a8aa860c2f5555ac4a81e4c027e5267]\n\nTherefore, the mixed number is [MATH: {\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{4}}]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nQuestion Graphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/q-56974]\n\nPrompt:\nWhat mixed number is shown on the number line?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 1(1)/(4)]\nb. [MATH: 2(3)/(4)]\nc. [MATH: 2(1)/(4)]\nd. [MATH: 1(1)/(2)]\ne. [MATH: 1(3)/(4)]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nQuestion Graphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/q-145911]\n\nPrompt:\nWhat mixed number is shown on the number line?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2(1)/(6)]\nb. [MATH: 1(1)/(6)]\nc. [MATH: (5)/(6)]\nd. [MATH: 2(4)/(5)]\ne. [MATH: 1(5)/(6)]"
